      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2848.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 685,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, and its role in the universe.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Structure\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* The atoms are held together by covalent bonds, sharing electrons. However, the oxygen atom pulls the shared electrons closer to itself than the hydrogen atoms do. This creates a \"polar\" molecule, meaning the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge.\n\n### 2. Physical States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, which fills the oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). \n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Unique and Marvelous Properties\nWater behaves very differently from most other liquids due to its molecular structure:\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Because water is polar, it can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients and how plants absorb minerals from the soil.\n* **Ice Floats (Density Anomaly):** When most liquids freeze, their molecules pack closer together, making the solid denser than the liquid. Water is the opposite. When water freezes, its molecules form an open hexagonal lattice, making ice *less dense* than liquid water. This is why ice floats, preventing lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, thus saving aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This moderates the Earth's climate and prevents coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (**cohesion**—which creates surface tension, allowing insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (**adhesion**—which allows water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 4. Water and Life\n* **Earth:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered in water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature via sweat, and provides the medium for nearly all cellular chemical reactions. Without water, biochemical processes simply cannot happen.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, shaping our geography, driving our weather systems, and making biological existence possible."
